<Guide lang="en" slug="buying-a-house-in-spain-bed-and-breakfast" toc={toc} faq={faq} dienst="aankoopkeuring" ctaTitle="Got a B&B in mind?" ctaText="We survey the building through an operator’s eyes: rooms, bathrooms, water, drainage and installations, with a price for the work needed. You get a report in English.">
  <p>This page is about taking over a B&amp;B, casa rural or small holiday letting business that is already running. If you want to start your own B&amp;B in a house you have yet to choose, read <a href="/en/guides/finca-apartment-or-village-house/bed-and-breakfast/">starting a B&amp;B in the Axarquía</a>. The normal buying steps are in our <a href="/en/guides/buying-a-house-in-spain/">step-by-step guide to buying a house in Spain</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="what">House or business: what are you buying?</h2>
  <p>A running B&amp;B is often priced above what the house alone would be worth. The seller counts the turnover, the reviews and the regular guests. That can be fair, but only if the turnover is real and survives the handover. So ask two separate questions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>What is the building worth as a home?</strong> Compare with ordinary houses nearby and include the cost of any work.</li>
    <li><strong>What does the business make, net</strong>, after all costs and a fair allowance for your own work? The gap between the asking price and the home value is what you pay for that result.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Usually you buy the building with its contents and start operating it yourself. If the B&amp;B is held in a Spanish company (SL) and you buy the shares, you also take on all the company’s debts and risks. Have the structure assessed by a lawyer and a gestor.</p>

  <h2 id="licence">Licence and land use</h2>
  <p>In Andalusia a B&amp;B is not a category of its own. It is a holiday home (VFT, also by the room), rural accommodation (such as a <em>casa rural</em>) or a small hotel type such as a guest house. Check:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The entry in the tourism register</strong> (<em>Registro de Turismo de Andalucía</em>): which category, how many rooms and how many guests. If the B&amp;B runs more rooms or guests than registered, part of the turnover cannot legally be repeated.</li>
    <li><strong>The national registration number</strong> required since July 2025 to advertise on rental platforms.</li>
    <li><strong>The legal status of the building.</strong> Do all guest rooms, bathrooms and the pool have permits? A finca with AFO status cannot be extended and tourist use is not a given. See <a href="/en/guides/afo-legalisation-andalusia/">AFO status and legalisation</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Local restrictions.</strong> Town halls can limit new holiday lets. Ask what applies when the operator changes.</li>
    <li><strong>Other permits</strong>: an activity licence for hotel-type businesses and, if you serve breakfast, food hygiene rules.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="accounts">Checking the accounts</h2>
  <p>Ask for at least three full years and the current year by month. Look at:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Turnover by month</strong>, so you see the season. Many B&amp;Bs in the Axarquía run from March to November and have few guests in winter.</li>
    <li><strong>Occupancy and average nightly rate</strong>, per room. High turnover at low prices is more fragile than lower turnover from regular guests who pay more.</li>
    <li><strong>All costs</strong>: platform commission, cleaning and laundry, energy and water, breakfast, maintenance, insurance, IBI, refuse tax, gestor, marketing and staff.</li>
    <li><strong>The seller’s own work.</strong> If the owners do everything themselves, their labour is not in the costs. Allow for it, or for the wages of someone who takes it over.</li>
    <li><strong>The trend.</strong> Is turnover rising or falling? Was one year a one-off?</li>
  </ul>
  <p><strong>Ask for proof.</strong> A spreadsheet is not evidence. Ask for the Spanish tax returns (income or corporation tax, and VAT), the payout statements from Booking.com, Airbnb and other channels, and bank statements. Do not count turnover said to be “cash” or off the books: you cannot verify it and cannot legally repeat it.</p>
  <p>Ask about <strong>future bookings</strong> too: how many are already in, and who gets the deposits guests have paid? Put it in the purchase contract.</p>

  <h2 id="channels">Booking channels and direct bookings</h2>
  <p>Where guests come from determines how much you keep and how fragile the turnover is. Ask for each channel’s share of bookings and revenue over recent years:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Platforms</strong> such as Booking.com, Airbnb and Expedia. They charge commission, often 15 to 20% at Booking.com and a comparable share of the total price at Airbnb. A B&amp;B that gets nearly everything through one platform depends on that platform’s algorithm and rules.</li>
    <li><strong>Direct bookings</strong> through its own website, phone or email. These earn the most. A high share of direct bookings and returning guests is often the most valuable part of a B&amp;B.</li>
    <li><strong>Other channels</strong>: tour operators, walking or cycling holidays, yoga retreats, weddings.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Ask what transfers. On Booking.com the listing belongs to the property and there is a procedure for a new owner. On Airbnb, reviews belong to the host’s account and usually do not move. You can only take over the list of direct guests if data protection rules (GDPR) allow it; have the lawyer arrange this.</p>

  <h2 id="online">Website, social media and reviews</h2>
  <p>A well-run B&amp;B gets found online. Look for yourself before asking the seller:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The website</strong>: is the domain in the seller’s name and does it transfer? How many visitors come and from where (Google, social media, platforms)? Ask for access to the statistics, such as Google Analytics and Search Console.</li>
    <li><strong>Google Business Profile</strong>: number of reviews, average rating and whether the owner replies.</li>
    <li><strong>Social media</strong>: which channels is the B&amp;B on (Instagram, Facebook, TikTok, YouTube)? How many followers, how often does it post and how much engagement does it get? An account with 10,000 followers that has been silent for a year is worth little. Ask whether the accounts transfer.</li>
    <li><strong>Reviews on every platform</strong>: read the last two years, not just the score. Recurring complaints about damp, noise, beds or the access road point to costs after the purchase.</li>
    <li><strong>The owner’s role.</strong> Guests often write about the host. If the B&amp;B runs on their personality, some regulars may not return after the handover.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="building">The building and installations</h2>
  <p>Guests put more strain on a house than residents. In a B&amp;B survey we look, beyond the usual points, at:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Each room’s bathroom: pipes, drainage, damp and ventilation.</li>
    <li>Hot water and water supply in general: can the source or connection cope with full occupancy in August?</li>
    <li>Drainage and septic tank: capacity and whether it meets the rules.</li>
    <li>Air conditioning and heating in every room, required for tourist lets in Andalusia.</li>
    <li>Electrics: capacity and safety, with air conditioning, kitchen and pool running at once.</li>
    <li>Fire safety, escape routes and sound insulation between rooms.</li>
    <li>Deferred maintenance: a seller who is winding down often invests little in the last few years.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>See also <a href="/en/guides/buying-a-finca-in-spain/">buying a finca in Spain</a> for water, access and drainage in the countryside, and guide prices in <a href="/en/guides/renovation-costs-spain/">renovation costs in Spain</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="staff">Staff and contracts</h2>
  <p>Does the B&amp;B employ staff, such as a cleaner or cook? If you take over the business as a whole, Spanish employment contracts generally transfer to the new owner, including accrued rights. Ask for a list of staff, contracts and any disputes. Look at running contracts with laundries, suppliers, tour operators and maintenance firms too: what continues, and what can you cancel?</p>

  <h2 id="compare">Comparing several B&amp;Bs</h2>
  <p>Ideally look at two or three candidates and put them side by side, converted to the same measures:</p>
  <div class="compare-wrap"><table class="compare">
    <thead><tr><th>Measure</th><th>Where to find it</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Asking price, and price per legal room</td><td>Listing and tourism register entry</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Home value without the business</td><td>Comparable houses nearby, valuation</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Turnover and net result over three years</td><td>Tax returns, platform payouts</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Occupancy and nightly rate by season</td><td>Monthly booking report</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Share of direct bookings and regulars</td><td>Booking report by channel</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Review score and trend</td><td>Google, Booking.com, Airbnb, Tripadvisor</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Online reach</td><td>Website statistics, social media</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Investment needed after purchase</td><td>Property survey with price estimate</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Legal status of building and licence</td><td>Lawyer, town hall, Junta</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Your own work required</td><td>Talking to the seller, staff list</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>For the B&amp;B that comes out best, draw up your own budget with a cautious scenario: lower occupancy in year one, platform commission and the investment from the survey. If it still works, you have a solid basis for your offer.</p>

  <h2 id="spanish">Questions in Spanish</h2>
  <p>A few questions to send to the seller or agent:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><q lang="es">¿Me puede enviar las declaraciones de impuestos y de IVA de los últimos tres años?</q> Could you send me the tax and VAT returns for the last three years?</li>
    <li><q lang="es">¿Me puede enviar los informes de pagos de Booking y Airbnb de los últimos tres años?</q> Could you send me the Booking and Airbnb payout reports for the last three years?</li>
    <li><q lang="es">¿Qué porcentaje de las reservas es directo y qué porcentaje viene de plataformas?</q> What share of bookings is direct and what share comes from platforms?</li>
    <li><q lang="es">¿Con qué número está inscrito en el Registro de Turismo de Andalucía, y para cuántas habitaciones y plazas?</q> Under which number are you registered with the tourism register, and for how many rooms and guests?</li>
    <li><q lang="es">¿Están incluidos en la venta el dominio web, las redes sociales y las reservas futuras?</q> Are the domain name, social media and future bookings included in the sale?</li>
    <li><q lang="es">¿Hay trabajadores contratados? ¿Me puede enviar sus contratos?</q> Are there employees? Could you send me their contracts?</li>
  </ul>
  <p>More questions about the house itself, in Spanish too, are in the <a href="/en/guides/buying-a-house-in-spain/checklist/">buying checklist</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="help">How we help</h2>
  <p>Have the accounts checked by a gestor or accountant, and the licence and structure by a lawyer. We do the building: our <a href="/en/property-survey/">property survey</a> looks at the B&amp;B through an operator’s eyes and prices the work needed, room by room. After the purchase we help with <a href="/en/renovation/">renovation</a>, <a href="/en/maintenance/">maintenance</a>, the <a href="/en/swimming-pool/">pool</a> and, when you are away, <a href="/en/key-holding/">key holding</a>. <a href="/en/request/?dienst=aankoopkeuring">Request a survey</a> or email <a href="mailto:info@axarquiahome.com">info@axarquiahome.com</a>.</p>

  <h2 id="sources">Sources</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Tourism in Andalusia: Ley 13/2011 del Turismo de Andalucía; holiday homes: Decreto 28/2016, amended by Decreto 31/2024; rural accommodation: Decreto 20/2002 de Turismo en el Medio Rural.</li>
    <li>National registration number for short-term lets: Real Decreto 1312/2024 (mandatory since 1 July 2025).</li>
    <li>Transfer of staff on a business takeover: Estatuto de los Trabajadores, article 44.</li>
    <li>Building on rural land and AFO: Ley 7/2021 (LISTA) and Decreto 550/2022.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="note">Checked on 5 November 2026. This guide gives general information and is not legal, tax or financial advice. Tourist letting rules change regularly; have a takeover handled by a lawyer and a gestor or accountant.</p>
